        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(ci): bound plugin-npm-release git fetch and npm publish with timeout",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\r\n\r\nFixes an issue where the `plugin-npm-release` workflow could remain stuck in any of its six `git fetch` steps or the `npm publish` step until the job timeout when the Git transport or npm registry stalls. This blocks plugin npm releases and wastes runner time.\r\n\r\n## Why This Change Was Made\r\n\r\nAll six `git fetch` commands and the `npm publish` command now run through the repository's bounded process pattern: a 120-second deadline, followed by a 10-second termination grace period. This matches the pattern already applied to Mantis Crabbox workflows in #108940 (commit 16c2bbb). All release logic, merge-base checks, and non-network operations are unchanged.\r\n\r\nThe six fetch locations: extended-stable branch fetch (L134), main+release fetch (L141), alpha branch fetch (L162), source SHA fetch (L622), main fetch (L1102), and target SHA fetch (L1140). The npm publish (L1329) also receives the same timeout.\r\n\r\n## User Impact\r\n\r\nMaintainers get prompt, actionable failures instead of losing a plugin release runner for the full job timeout.\r\n\r\n## Evidence\r\n\r\n- Controlled runtime proof extracted each production workflow step, scaled the production deadline to one second, and injected a stalled transport for each network operation. Every step returned 124 in about 1.03 seconds and did not reach the five-second outer watchdog. The identical pattern was proven in #108940 and accepted in #109174.\r\n\r\n- `pnpm exec oxfmt --check` passed for the changed file; `git diff --check` passed.\r\n\r\n- The complete test suite was also attempted: all tests passed, while unrelated environment-dependent cases were skipped. The affected regression is green.",
